               <code>
                  <coding>
                     <system value="http://loinc.org"/>
                     <code value="8648-8"/>
                     <display value="Hospital course note"/>
                  </coding>
               </code>
               <text>
                  <status value="generated"/>
                  <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent hospital admission of your patient, a 75-year-old male, who presented on 09/04/1974 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically consistent with acute appendicitis. Physical examination revealed marked tenderness at McBurney's point with rebound tenderness. Laboratory investigations confirmed leukocytosis, and abdominal ultrasound supported the diagnosis of acute appendicitis. The patient was resuscitated with intravenous fluids and commenced on intravenous antibiotics. Given his age and clinical condition, a laparoscopic appendectomy was successfully performed without intraoperative complications. Postoperative recovery was uneventful. Wound care was provided, and vital signs remained stable throughout. The patient was mobilized with physiotherapy support and discharged home on 11/04/1974 in satisfactory condition. Please continue routine postoperative follow-up and wound monitoring. Do not hesitate to contact me with any queries.</div>
               </text>
            </section>
